{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"cummy-hurtful-firefly","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Summary\n\n`terraform-drift.yml` has been failing with a non-empty plan: **1 to add, 1 to change, 11 to\ndestroy**. All 11 destroys are the complete Bedrock spend-cap subsystem — the mechanism that\nmeters spend and raises the operator's daily-spend alert.\n\nThe cause is not live infrastructure drifting away from code. It is the opposite: the\nsubsystem was applied to production from a branch whose HCL **never landed on `main`**.\nTerraform state holds the resources, production runs them, and `main` does not declare them —\nso a plan computed against `main` correctly concludes they should be destroyed.\n\n## Evidence\n\nResources the plan destroys (from run 35315843850, job 105507194706):\n\n- `aws_budgets_budget.bedrock_daily`\n- `aws_cloudwatch_event_rule.bedrock_spend_cap_tick`\n- `aws_cloudwatch_event_target.bedrock_spend_cap_tick`\n- `aws_cloudwatch_log_group.bedrock_spend_cap`\n- `aws_iam_policy.bedrock_spend_cap_deny`\n- `aws_iam_role.bedrock_spend_cap`\n- `aws_iam_role_policy.bedrock_spend_cap`\n- `aws_lambda_function.bedrock_spend_cap`\n- `aws_lambda_permission.bedrock_spend_cap_tick`\n- `aws_sns_topic.bedrock_spend_cap_alerts`\n- `aws_sns_topic_policy.bedrock_spend_cap_alerts`\n\nThe HCL is absent from `main` and present only on an unlanded branch:\n\n```\n$ git grep -l 'bedrock_spend_cap' origin/main -- 'infra/**/*.tf'\n(no output)\n\n$ git grep -l 'bedrock_spend_cap' <all refs> -- 'infra/**'\nrefs/remotes/origin/bedrock-daily-spend-cap:infra/terraform/bedrock-spend-cap/README.md\nrefs/remotes/origin/bedrock-daily-spend-cap:infra/terraform/bedrock-spend-cap/handler.py\nrefs/remotes/origin/bedrock-daily-spend-cap:infra/terraform/bedrock_spend_cap.tf\n```\n\nThe branch carries 2 commits and 1,063 insertions across those 3 files, and is pushed to\n`origin` but has no Gerrit change and is not merged.\n\nThe plan's 1 create is separate and opposite: `aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm.reviewbot_webhook_auth_rejections`\nis declared in `infra/terraform/monitoring_autodeploy.tf` on `main` but has never been applied.\nThe 1 in-place change is `aws_instance.gerrit`.\n\n## Why this is P1\n\nAnyone who runs `terraform apply` from `main` — including any automated reconciliation — will\n**delete the spend metering and alerting subsystem** while believing they are reconciling\ndrift. The protection is load-bearing for agent operations: the standing delivery loop reads\nthe meter from this Lambda's log output on every tick and pauses all work when its alert\nfires. Destroying it removes the guard silently, and the loop would go on reading a meter that\nno longer exists.\n\nSecondarily, the drift lane is a monitoring capability that is currently saturated by this one\nknown condition, so a genuine second drift would not be distinguishable.\n\n## Publication constraint on the fix\n\nThe branch's commit subject contains a literal currency amount. This repository and its ticket\nstore are public, and project policy is that monetary figures do not go into committed\nguidance or history. Landing the branch unmodified would write that figure permanently into\n`main`'s public history. The commit messages must be rewritten before the change is pushed for\nreview; the threshold belongs in the HCL/variable it configures, not in prose that describes it.\n\nThe same check applies to the branch's `README.md` and any comments in `bedrock_spend_cap.tf`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 The spend-cap HCL is present on `main`, landed through Gerrit per `CONTRIBUTING.md`, with `LLM-Review +1` and `Verified +1`.\n- [ ] AC2 No commit message, README, or HCL comment introduced by that change contains a currency amount. Checked: `git log origin/main -p <files> | grep -nE '\\$[0-9]'` returns nothing.\n- [ ] AC3 The landed HCL matches the live resources, so `terraform plan` proposes zero destroys for all 11 resources listed above. Checked: read the plan output in the drift run.\n- [ ] AC4 A decision is recorded for `aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm.reviewbot_webhook_auth_rejections` — either applied so the plan is empty, or removed from HCL if unwanted.\n- [ ] AC5 A decision is recorded for the `aws_instance.gerrit` in-place change.\n- [ ] AC6 `terraform-drift.yml` completes green on a scheduled run, with an empty plan.\n- [ ] AC7 The root cause is addressed, not just the instance: applying to production from an unlanded branch is what created a state/HCL divergence that took days to surface. Record how that is prevented or detected.\n\n## Testing\n\n`terraform plan` in the drift workflow is the proving command; an empty plan is the pass\ncondition. Confirm with a dispatched run of `terraform-drift.yml` after the change lands,\nand re-read the job log rather than trusting the workflow's summary line.\n","id":"8d27-d715-0611-48e3","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Terraform drift: the entire Bedrock spend-cap subsystem is live and in state but its HCL was never landed on main, so the drift lane plans to destroy 11 resources"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1789715370584457962,"uuid":"961db406-5612-4418-989a-9fb6f01f500b"}